<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Benevento</h1><p class="st-meta">571 CE – 1077 CE (approx.) · capital Benevento</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Southern Lombard duchy and later principality that outlived the Lombard Kingdom by three centuries, a centre of southern Italian culture.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> warriors under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zotto">Zotto</a> established themselves at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benevento">Beneventum</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/campania">Campania</a> around 571, perhaps first as soldiers in Byzantine pay. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arechis-i-of-benevento">Arechis I</a> the duchy expanded across most of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, apart from the Byzantine coastal cities, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calabria">Calabria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Apulia's</a> heel.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benevento">Benevento</a> was effectively independent of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/commune-of-pavia">Pavia</a>; Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grimoald-i-of-benevento">Grimoald I</a> even became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-of-the-lombards">King of the Lombards</a> in 662. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> took the north in 774, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arechis-ii-of-benevento">Arechis II</a> took the title of prince and claimed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> heritage, building the church of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santa-sofia-benevento">Santa Sofia</a>. The duchy developed the Beneventan script and was a cultural centre linked to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monte-cassino">Monte Cassino</a>. Civil war in 849 split it into Benevento and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-salerno">Salerno</a>, and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-capua">Capua</a> later broke away.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Weakened by Arab raids, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> and Normans, the principality shrank. In 1051 the city placed itself under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-leo-ix">Pope Leo IX</a>, and when the last prince, Landulf VI, died in 1077, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benevento">Benevento</a> became a papal enclave, while its lands passed to the Normans.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-benevento.jpg" alt="Duchy of Benevento" width="320" height="218"><figcaption>Image: The original map was reconstructed by Alfredo Zazo and sculpted by Michelangelo Parlato. · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Duchy_of_Benevento_It.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>571 CE – 1077 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Benevento</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Zotto, Grimoald I, Arechis II</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Lombardic, Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Principality of Benevento, Ducatus Beneventanus</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=824&amp;focus=duchy-of-benevento" aria-label="Duchy of Benevento on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5041 710 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Benevento lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5361 966L5218 839M5361 966L5139 672M5361 966L5304 942M5361 966L5362 981M5361 966L5349 965"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5218 839h0M5139 672h0M5304 942h0M5362 981h0M5349 965h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5361 966h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5361 966h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Benevento. <a href="/atlas?year=824&amp;focus=duchy-of-benevento">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
